{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'rg -n \"orange|color|colour|markup\" docs/source/style.rst docs/source/markup.rst rich/style.py rich/console.py | head -80'","aggregated_output":"docs/source/markup.rst:1:.. _console_markup:\ndocs/source/markup.rst:6:Rich supports a simple markup which you can use to insert color and styles virtually everywhere Rich would accept a string (e.g. :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` and :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log`).\ndocs/source/markup.rst:10:    python -m rich.markup\ndocs/source/markup.rst:15:Console markup uses a syntax inspired by `bbcode <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BBCode>`_. If you write the style (see :ref:`styles`) in square brackets, e.g. ``[bold red]``, that style will apply until it is *closed* with a corresponding ``[/bold red]``.\ndocs/source/markup.rst:30:These markup tags may be use in combination with each other and don't need to be strictly nested. The following example demonstrates overlapping of markup tags:: \ndocs/source/markup.rst:37:Rich will raise :class:`~rich.errors.MarkupError` if the markup contains one of the following errors:\ndocs/source/markup.rst:46:Console markup can output hyperlinks with the following syntax: ``[link=URL]text[/link]``. Here's an example::\ndocs/source/markup.rst:56:Occasionally you may want to print something that Rich would interpret as markup. You can *escape* a tag by preceding it with a backslash. Here's an example::\ndocs/source/markup.rst:67:The function :func:`~rich.markup.escape` will handle escaping of text for you.\ndocs/source/markup.rst:69:Escaping is important if you construct console markup dynamically, with ``str.format`` or f strings (for example). Without escaping it may be possible to inject tags where you don't want them. Consider the following function::\ndocs/source/markup.rst:76:    from rich.markup import escape\ndocs/source/markup.rst:83:If you add an *emoji code* to markup it will be replaced with the equivalent unicode character. An emoji code consists of the name of the emoji surrounded be colons (:). Here's an example::\ndocs/source/markup.rst:89:Some emojis have two variants, the \"emoji\" variant displays in full color, and the \"text\" variant displays in monochrome (whatever your default colors are set to). You can specify the variant you want by adding either `\"-emoji\"` or `\"-text\"` to the emoji code. Here's an example:\ndocs/source/markup.rst:103:By default, Rich will render console markup when you explicitly pass a string to :meth:`~rich.console.Print.print` or implicitly when you embed a string in another renderable object such as :class:`~rich.table.Table` or :class:`~rich.panel.Panel`.\ndocs/source/markup.rst:105:Console markup is convenient, but you may wish to disable it if the syntax clashes with the string you want to print. You can do this by setting ``markup=False`` on the :meth:`~rich.console.Print.print` method or on the :class:`~rich.console.Console` constructor.\ndocs/source/markup.rst:111:You can convert a string to styled text by calling :meth:`~rich.text.Text.from_markup`, which returns a :class:`~rich.text.Text` instance you can print or add more styles to.\nrich/console.py:46:from .color import ColorSystem, blend_rgb\nrich/console.py:50:from .markup import render as render_markup\nrich/console.py:104:    \"256color\": ColorSystem.EIGHT_BIT,\nrich/console.py:105:    \"16color\": ColorSystem.STANDARD,\nrich/console.py:144:    markup: Optional[bool] = None\nrich/console.py:145:    \"\"\"Enable markup when rendering strings.\"\"\"\nrich/console.py:173:        markup: Union[Optional[bool], NoChange] = NO_CHANGE,\nrich/console.py:192:        if not isinstance(markup, NoChange):\nrich/console.py:193:            options.markup = markup\nrich/console.py:534:    \"truecolor\": ColorSystem.TRUECOLOR,\nrich/console.py:591:        color_system (str, optional): The color system supported by your terminal,\nrich/console.py:592:            either ``\"standard\"``, ``\"256\"`` or ``\"truecolor\"``. Leave as ``\"auto\"`` to autodetect.\nrich/console.py:604:        no_color (Optional[bool], optional): Enabled no color mode, or None to auto detect. Defaults to None.\nrich/console.py:608:        markup (bool, optional): Boolean to enable :ref:`console_markup`. Defaults to True.\nrich/console.py:628:        color_system: Optional[\nrich/console.py:629:            Literal[\"auto\", \"standard\", \"256\", \"truecolor\", \"windows\"]\nrich/console.py:642:        no_color: Optional[bool] = None,\nrich/console.py:645:        markup: bool = True,\nrich/console.py:680:        self._markup = markup\nrich/console.py:703:        self._color_system: Optional[ColorSystem]\nrich/console.py:713:        if color_system is None:\nrich/console.py:714:            self._color_system = None\nrich/console.py:715:        elif color_system == \"auto\":\nrich/console.py:716:            self._color_system = self._detect_color_system()\nrich/console.py:718:            self._color_system = COLOR_SYSTEMS[color_system]\nrich/console.py:731:        self.no_color = (\nrich/console.py:732:            no_color\nrich/console.py:733:            if no_color is not None\nrich/console.py:760:        return f\"<console width={self.width} {self._color_system!s}>\"\nrich/console.py:795:    def _detect_color_system(self) -> Optional[ColorSystem]:\nrich/console.py:796:        \"\"\"Detect color system from env vars.\"\"\"\nrich/console.py:807:                if windows_console_features.truecolor\nrich/console.py:811:            color_term = self._environ.get(\"COLORTERM\", \"\").strip().lower()\nrich/console.py:812:            if color_term in (\"truecolor\", \"24bit\"):\nrich/console.py:815:            _term_name, _hyphen, colors = term.rpartition(\"-\")\nrich/console.py:816:            color_system = _TERM_COLORS.get(colors, ColorSystem.STANDARD)\nrich/console.py:817:            return color_system\nrich/console.py:915:    def color_system(self) -> Optional[str]:\nrich/console.py:916:        \"\"\"Get color system string.\nrich/console.py:919:            Optional[str]: \"standard\", \"256\" or \"truecolor\".\nrich/console.py:922:        if self._color_system is not None:\nrich/console.py:923:            return _COLOR_SYSTEMS_NAMES[self._color_system]\nrich/console.py:969:        # https://force-color.org/\nrich/console.py:970:        force_color = environ.get(\"FORCE_COLOR\")\nrich/console.py:971:        if force_color is not None:\nrich/console.py:972:            return force_color != \"\"\nrich/console.py:1328:                renderable, highlight=_options.highlight, markup=_options.markup\nrich/console.py:1423:        markup: Optional[bool] = None,\nrich/console.py:1436:            markup (Optional[bool], optional): Enable markup, or ``None`` to use Console default.\nrich/console.py:1444:        markup_enabled = markup or (markup is None and self._markup)\nrich/console.py:1447:        if markup_enabled:\nrich/console.py:1448:            rich_text = render_markup(\nrich/console.py:1514:        markup: Optional[bool] = None,\nrich/console.py:1525:            markup (Optional[bool], optional): Enable markup, or ``None`` to use console default.\nrich/console.py:1561:                        markup=markup,\nrich/console.py:1625:        :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` won't pretty print, wrap text, or apply markup, but will\nrich/console.py:1641:            markup=False,\nrich/console.py:1658:        markup: Optional[bool] = None,\nrich/console.py:1677:            markup (Optional[bool], optional): Enable markup, or ``None`` to use console default. Defaults to ``None``.\nrich/console.py:1704:                markup=markup,\nrich/console.py:1715:                markup=markup,\nrich/console.py:1944:        markup: Optional[bool] = None,\nrich/console.py:1958:            markup (Optional[bool], optional): Enable markup, or ``None`` to use console default. Defaults to None.\nrich/console.py:1976:       